{
  // Rules disabled for this corpus. markdownlint reads this file as JSONC, so the
  // reasoning lives next to the decision rather than in a commit nobody re-reads.
  "MD013": false,
  "MD024": false,

  // MD025 (multiple top-level headings) disabled by #7927. 10 errors across 9 files.
  // The argument is COHERENCE, not volume: MD041 (first line must be a top-level
  // heading) is already off, so the corpus does not promise that a document opens with
  // one H1 -- and enforcing "at most one H1" over documents that need not begin with an
  // H1 at all polices a structure nothing else asserts. The two rules are a pair; keeping
  // one without the other is arbitrary. Post-mortems and audits legitimately carry
  // several top-level sections.
  //
  // MD001 (heading-increment) was proposed for removal alongside it and is DELIBERATELY
  // KEPT. The plan bundled the two on a measured "116 errors" figure; re-measured at the
  // gate, MD025 and MD001 together are 15 (10 + 5), so volume never justified either
  // removal. MD001 has no MD041-style coherence argument -- a heading that jumps h2 to h4
  // is a real document-structure defect -- and 5 sites is a cheap fix, not a rule to
  // switch off. Fixed by hand instead.
  "MD025": false,

  // MD018 (no-missing-space-atx) disabled by #7927, and this one is not a style call --
  // its FIX IS DESTRUCTIVE on this corpus. The rule assumes a line starting `#Text` is a
  // heading someone forgot the space in. Here it is prose: an issue reference (`#5913`)
  // or a channel (`#testing`) that happens to begin a wrapped line. CommonMark agrees
  // with the prose reading -- ATX headings REQUIRE the space, so `#5913` is already a
  // paragraph and there is nothing to repair.
  //
  // Measured, not supposed: all 106 hits in the swept corpus are prose, 105 of them
  // `#<digits>`. A --fix pass ran with only MD037/038/049/050 withheld and converted 106
  // of them into real headings across 78 files, silently turning sentence fragments into
  // H1s -- one mid-sentence continuation line became a top-level heading with blank lines
  // inserted around it. The tell was 18 NEW MD001 heading-increment errors appearing in a
  // sweep that was supposed to be whitespace-only.
  //
  // A word-sequence check does NOT catch this: inserting a space after `#` merges and
  // splits nothing, so the tokens are identical. The guard that catches it is heading
  // COUNT, which the sweep now asserts is unchanged per file.
  "MD018": false,

  "MD026": false,
  "MD029": false,
  "MD033": false,
  "MD036": false,
  "MD040": false,
  "MD041": false,
  "MD056": false,
  "MD060": false
}
